  "question": {
    "text": "<p>Given below are two statements :<br /><strong>Statement I :</strong> A uniform wire of resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>80</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math>&nbsp;is cut into four equal parts. These parts are now connected in parallel. The equivalent resistance of the combination will be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math>.</p><p><strong>Statement II :</strong> Two resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>R</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mi>R</mi></math> are connected in parallel in an electric circuit. The value of thermal energy developed in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mi>R</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>R</mi></math> will be in the ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>2</mn></math>.</p><p>In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below</p>"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Both statement I and statement II are correct",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Both statement I and statement II are incorrect",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "Statement I is incorrect but statement II is correct.",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "C"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p><strong>Statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo></math></strong>&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>80</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math></p><p>When it is cut&nbsp;into&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math>&nbsp;equal parts, the resistance of each part will be,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math></p><p>When these resistances are connected in parallel,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>eq</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>20</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math></p><p>Hence, the&nbsp;statement I is correct.</p><p><strong>Statement 2 -</strong></p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0121_192b0a6de933.png\" style=\"width: 120px; height: 144px;\" /></p><p>When the resistances are connected in parallel, the potential difference between them is same. Therefore, the power consumed can be written as,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>R</mi></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mfrac><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>Hence, statement II is incorrect.</p></blockquote>"
  }
}